 # Cube-3 Hybrid Ray-Casting
 
-Also called: Cube-3, Cube-3 Architecture, Cubic Frame Buffer rendering
+Also called: Cube-3 algorithm
 
 ## Overview
-Cube-3 is a highly parallel, pipelined hardware architecture and hybrid ray-casting algorithm designed for the real-time visualization of high-resolution volumetric datasets (e.g., $512^3$ voxels) [S1]. It solves the traditional memory access bottleneck in volume rendering by ensuring at most one memory access per voxel per projection without pre-processing or data-dependent optimizations [S1]. Unlike object-order methods like VIRIM or image-order methods like VOGUE, which process single ray samples per pipeline stage, Cube-3 processes all sample values belonging to a ray simultaneously, exploiting spatial coherency to achieve the largest possible degree of parallelism [S1].
+Cube-3 is a hardware-oriented hybrid ray-casting algorithm designed for the real-time visualization of high-resolution volumetric datasets (e.g., $512^3$ voxels) [S4]. It solves the traditional memory access bottleneck in volume rendering by ensuring that each voxel is read from memory at most once per projection, without pre-processing or data-dependent optimizations [S4]. Unlike object-order methods like VIRIM or image-order methods like VOGUE, Cube-3 exploits spatial coherency by processing all sample values of a ray simultaneously in a fully pipelined architecture [S4]. It is distinguished by its use of a Cubic Frame Buffer (CFB) with skewed memory organization and a global communication network (Fast Bus) to align discrete rays for parallel processing [S3, unofficial][S4].
 
 ## How it works
-The method combines discrete ray tracing with hybrid interpolation to generate continuous ray samples from a skewed memory structure, shading them, and compositing them via a hierarchical tree [S1].
+The algorithm combines object-order data access with image-order projection. It generates discrete voxel-rays from continuous viewing rays, fetches them as beams from a skewed memory structure, and composites them into a base-plane image, which is subsequently warped to the final view plane [S4].
 
 ### Inputs and Outputs
-*   **Input:** A volumetric dataset of size $n^3$ voxels, where each voxel is 16 bits. The viewing direction defines a "base-plane" (the volume face most perpendicular to the view) [S1].
-*   **Output:** A projected image on the base-plane, which is subsequently warped and interpolated by the host into the final view plane image [S1].
-
